  └─────────┴──────────┴─────────┴─────────┘
  (Per-device, per-window breakdowns live in each allowance subfolder's imputation_counts.log.)

Interpretation
--------------
  Increasing the watermark allowance from 0s to 10s has NO meaningful effect on the
  imputation rate (~29% throughout).  The input streams have a mean event lateness of
  roughly 50s (max ~180-256s), which dwarfs the 0-10s allowances tested, so almost all
  out-of-order events are dropped regardless.  Meaningfully reducing imputation would
  require an allowance on the order of the disorder itself (~50s+), which would in turn
  add that same delay to every window's end-to-end latency — the fundamental
  latency-vs-completeness tradeoff of event-time windowing.

Note on row counts: total rows differ (2700 vs 3000) only because the staggered-start runs
captured a different number of complete 60-row windows before the experiment ended, not
because of the allowance itself.

Sources:
  ./0sec_watermark/imputation_counts.log
  ./5sec_watermark/imputation_counts.log
  ./10sec_watermark/imputation_counts.log
